\description{
Formats treatment effects (average treatment effects and
potential-outcome means) or margins (predictive margins and average
marginal effects) as a table with one estimate, confidence interval and
p-value block per model. The estimates come from the
\href{https://marginaleffects.com}{marginaleffects} package, from a data frame,
or from a matrix; \code{effecttab()} only formats them. R implementation of
the Stata \code{effecttab} command (tables of Stata's \code{teffects} and
\code{margins} results); the table prints, and is written to Excel with the
tabtools house style, and to CSV and Markdown.
}
\section{Input}{

Each argument in \code{...} is one model (a column block of the table), or a
single unnamed list holds one model per element (as in \code{\link[=regtab]{regtab()}}; its
names become the model labels). A model is
\itemize{
\item a marginaleffects result: \code{\link[marginaleffects:avg_predictions]{marginaleffects::avg_predictions()}},
\code{\link[marginaleffects:avg_comparisons]{marginaleffects::avg_comparisons()}} or \code{\link[marginaleffects:avg_slopes]{marginaleffects::avg_slopes()}}
(averaged results; \code{by} subgroups of a comparison are not read). A
result with a \verb{hypothesis =} test, and a \code{\link[marginaleffects:hypotheses]{marginaleffects::hypotheses()}}
result, give one margins row per hypothesis, labelled by it (\code{b2 - b1},
\code{Manual - Automatic});
\item a data frame with one row per effect: \code{estimate} and a \code{term} naming
the row, plus \code{conf.low}/\code{conf.high} and \code{p.value} (or \code{std.error},
from which a Wald interval and p-value are derived; the p-value tests
an optional \code{null.value} column, default 0, and a ratio header such as
\code{effect = "RR"} without \code{null.value} or \code{p.value} is refused). Both
bounds or neither: a row with one is refused, and \code{conf.low} must not
exceed \code{conf.high}. A \code{term} may be a
Stata key, as in \code{r(table)}: \code{r1vs0.mbsmoke} (a contrast), \verb{0.mbsmoke}
(a potential-outcome mean, or a factor level under \code{type = "margins"}), \verb{1b.race} (a base level), \code{o.x} (omitted), \code{age}; an
\code{equation} column (\code{ATE}, \code{ATET}, \code{POmean}) keeps Stata's teffects
equations and drops the treatment and outcome models' coefficients. A
row may instead be described by \code{kind} (\code{"contrast"}, \code{"pomean"},
\code{"prediction"}, \code{"slope"}, \code{"level"}, \code{"ref"}), \code{variable}, \code{level} and
\code{base}, or labelled directly by a \code{label} column. Optional: \code{status}
(\code{"base"}, \code{"omit"}, \code{"empty"}), and the attributes \code{conf.level} and
\code{tt_estimator} (a teffects estimator such as \code{"ipw"});
\item a numeric matrix with at least four columns, the estimate, the lower
and upper bounds, and the p-value (Stata's \code{from()}): its row names are
the labels, with \verb{_} shown as a space;
\item an unnamed list of marginaleffects results or data frames, stacked into
one model: a teffects model is the contrast and its potential-outcome
mean, e.g. \code{list(avg_comparisons(fit, variables = "treat"), avg_predictions(fit, variables = list(treat = 0)))}. One such model is
\code{effecttab(list(ate, po))}: a single unnamed list of exactly one
comparison and one prediction of the same variable is read as one
treatment-effect model, not as two models (with \code{type = "margins"} it
is two). Several are \code{effecttab(list(IPW = list(ate, po), RA = list(ate2, po2)))}, or named arguments \code{effecttab(IPW = list(ate, po))}.
}

Rows of several models are joined by their keys, and a factor's levels
by level: a plain factor's or a character variable's levels are keyed
as \code{\link[=regtab]{regtab()}} keys them, a level whose text is an integer by that
integer (\code{factor(0:2)} is 0, 1, 2) and any other level by position, so a
factor relevelled in one model, or a subset lacking a level, is recoded
to one numbering over every model, as \code{\link[=regtab]{regtab()}} does.
A variable coded by position in one model and by value in another (a
plain factor beside a \code{\link[=tt_as_factor]{tt_as_factor()}} or haven-labelled one) whose keys
then name different levels is refused with an error of class
\code{tabtools_error_effect_row_mismatch}. A continuous comparison other than
the derivative (\code{+1}, \code{sd}) is its own row, so the \code{+1} and \code{+2}
contrasts of two models are two rows.
}

\section{Teffects and margins}{

\code{type = "auto"} makes a table of treatment effects when a model stacks a
comparison and a prediction (a contrast and its potential-outcome mean),
or when a data frame holds teffects rows, and a margins table otherwise;
teffects and margins results cannot be mixed. In a teffects table,
comparisons are treatment contrasts and predictions potential-outcome
means. Without \code{clean} they show Stata's raw keys, \code{r1vs0.mbsmoke} and
\verb{0.mbsmoke}, built from the treatment's level codes (a value-labelled or
numeric variable's values; a factor from \code{\link[=tt_as_factor]{tt_as_factor()}} keeps its
Stata codes; any other factor is coded by its integer level texts, else
by level position). With \code{clean}
(or \code{tlabels}) they read \verb{Smoker vs Nonsmoker} and \verb{Nonsmoker (PO Mean)}
when the levels have labels, else \verb{1 if mother smoked (1 vs 0)} and \verb{1 if mother smoked = 0 (PO Mean)}, from the variable label with its first
letter capitalised. \code{tlabels} replaces the value labels: a level it does
not name shows its code. Stata's \verb{teffects ..., ate} reports the control
group's potential-outcome mean only; \code{avg_predictions(fit, variables = list(treat = 0))} gives that row alone.

In a margins table a factor (a factor, character or logical variable in
the model) is headed by its variable label with its levels indented
beneath, a comparison's base level shown as \code{refcat}; a continuous
variable is one row labelled by its variable label, with the contrast
appended when it is not the derivative (\code{Age (years) (+10)}). Rows
follow the order the variables were requested in, as Stata lists
\code{dydx()} terms, when \code{variables} is written out in the call (\code{variables = c("b", "a")}); \code{variables = vars} keeps marginaleffects' alphabetical
order. A multi-valued treatment's contrasts are listed in level order.
Labels come from \code{data}, else from the model's data. A \code{tlabels} level
of a plain factor (one without Stata codes) may be named by its level,
and an unnamed level of such a factor keeps its level text.
}

\section{Ratios (risk ratios after IPTW)}{

The recommended route to a risk ratio is the log ratio, exponentiated:
\code{avg_comparisons(fit, variables = "treat", comparison = "lnratioavg", transform = exp)} (or \code{"lnoravg"} for an odds ratio), with its
potential-outcome mean in a teffects model. The p-value is marginaleffects'
Wald test of the log ratio against 0, that is of the ratio against 1, and
the interval is the exponentiated log-scale interval. This is how Stata
reports ratios after \code{teffects} (\code{nlcom} or \code{margins} on the log scale,
then exponentiated): the interval respects the ratio's range and the test
and interval agree.

A plain ratio (\code{comparison = "ratio"}) carries a delta-method standard
error on the ratio scale, and marginaleffects tests it against 0, which
is not the null a ratio table displays. \code{effecttab()} therefore
recomputes that p-value against 1, z = (ratio - 1) / se (t with the
result's df when finite), keeps marginaleffects' interval, and adds "P-values of ratios test a
ratio of 1." to the footnote. A result that states a numeric
\code{hypothesis} keeps marginaleffects' test of that null, unchanged; a null
other than 1 (0 for a difference) is then the one the footnote names.
Its interval, symmetric on the ratio scale, can reach below 0; such a
result gives a warning and a footnote pointing to the log ratio. If a
plain-ratio result has a p-value but no usable ratio-scale standard
error (for example after \code{transform}), its default-null test cannot be
recomputed and is refused. Compute it with \code{hypothesis = 1} before any
transformation, or use the log-ratio route above. In a
table of several models a note that holds for some models only names
them (\verb{Model 2: P-values test a null value of 2.}).
}

\section{Confidence level}{

The level is the results' own: marginaleffects records it, and a data
frame may carry it (\code{attr(x, "conf.level")}). All results must agree,
and \code{level}, if given, must match them. A result that records no level
uses \code{level}, else 95\% (with a message); a matrix uses \code{level}, else 95\%,
silently, as Stata's \code{from()} does. Intervals a data frame leaves to be
derived from \code{std.error} are built at its own level, else at \code{level},
else at 95\%, from the normal distribution or Student's t with the row's
\code{df} (\code{df.error}), so the header always names the interval's level.
Derived intervals and p-values remain missing for a non-finite estimate
or standard error, or an explicitly missing \code{df}. An absent \code{df} column
or \code{df = Inf} uses the normal distribution. Supplied inference is kept.
}

\section{Differences from Stata}{

\itemize{
\item Stata reads the active collection; R takes the results as arguments,
and no file is required. R has no frames: the returned table (and
\code{\link[=as.data.frame]{as.data.frame()}} of it) stands for \code{frame()}, \code{\link[=as_forest_data]{as_forest_data()}} for
\code{eplotframe()}.
\item \code{full} (the treatment and outcome models' coefficients) is not ported:
use \code{\link[=regtab]{regtab()}} on those models.
\item Stata shows \verb{margins, at()} and \code{over()} results by their raw keys
(\verb{1._at}, \verb{1.sex#0.highbp}); R shows the values and value labels under
the variable (\code{Age (years)}, \code{  40}; \code{sex#highbp}, \code{  Male#0}). A
data frame with Stata's keys reproduces Stata's rows.
\item A Reference row comes from the structure (a comparison's base level, a
\verb{1b.} key, \code{status = "base"}), never from a zero estimate: Stata
falls back to showing a zero estimate without an interval as the
reference when its collection records no base level.
\item The methods sentence names the marginaleffects package for a
marginaleffects margins result, and drops Stata's closing "Analysis
performed in Stata ..." sentence.
\item Like \code{\link[=regtab]{regtab()}}, R prints no \verb{Exported to ...} or \verb{Markdown exported to ...} lines; \code{level} also takes a proportion.
\item The methods sentence names what the table holds: "Potential-outcome
means ..." for \code{pomeans}, "Average treatment effects on the treated
..." for an ATET (a data frame's \code{ATET} equation, or a
\code{WeightIt::glm_weightit()} fit with \code{estimand = "ATT"}), where Stata says
"Average treatment effects" for all three. The estimator of a
\code{glm_weightit()} fit with logistic weights is read from it (\code{ipw} for
\code{y ~ treat}, else \code{ipwra}). Other results cannot say which effect they
estimate (an \code{avg_comparisons()} over the treated, or of a fit with
ATT weights), so \code{estimand} (R only) states it.
\item \strong{Regression adjustment standard errors.} \verb{teffects ra} reports a
robust (sandwich) variance. \code{lm()}/\code{glm()} results passed to
marginaleffects with its default \code{vcov} use the model-based variance,
which is narrower: on Stata's \code{cattaneo2} example the ATE's standard
error is 1.8\% smaller. Pass \code{vcov = "HC0"}; the gap is then
about 0.1\%, the part \code{teffects} adds for the sampling variation of the
covariates (the delta method treats them as fixed). \code{effecttab()} says
so once per session when a treatment-effect result comes from an
\code{lm()}/\code{glm()} fit with the default variance.
\item \strong{0/1 variables in marginal effects.} marginaleffects always reports
the discrete change 1 - 0 of a 0/1 variable (also with
\code{avg_slopes()}), which is Stata's \code{dydx(i.b)}; Stata's \code{dydx(b)}
without \code{i.} is the derivative at the observed values. The two can be
far apart: about 9\% for a logit coefficient of 1.5 at a baseline risk
near 30\% (0.1-0.3\% where the effects are small). For
parity use \code{dydx(i.b)} in Stata; \code{effecttab()} says so once per
session.
}
}

\examples{
if (requireNamespace("marginaleffects", quietly = TRUE)) {
  d <- mtcars
  d$am <- factor(d$am, 0:1, c("Automatic", "Manual"))
  attr(d$am, "label") <- "Transmission"
  fit <- glm(vs ~ am + wt, family = binomial, data = d)
  # A treatment contrast (risk difference) and the control group's
  # potential-outcome mean, as one treatment-effect model
  ate <- marginaleffects::avg_comparisons(fit, variables = "am")
  po <- marginaleffects::avg_predictions(fit, variables = list(am = "Automatic"))
  print(effecttab(list(ate, po), clean = TRUE, effect = "RD"))
  # Predictive margins, and average marginal effects of two variables
  print(effecttab(marginaleffects::avg_predictions(fit, variables = "am"),
                  effect = "Pr(straight engine)"))
  print(effecttab(marginaleffects::avg_comparisons(fit, variables = "am"),
                  marginaleffects::avg_slopes(fit, variables = "wt"),
                  effect = "AME", models = c("Transmission", "Weight")))
}

# Estimates from elsewhere, as a data frame: here Stata's r(table) after
# `teffects ipw ..., ate`
rt <- data.frame(equation = c("ATE", "POmean"),
                 term = c("r1vs0.smoke", "0.smoke"),
                 estimate = c(-262.98, 3406.38),
                 conf.low = c(-307.69, 3387.86), conf.high = c(-218.27, 3424.90),
                 p.value = c(9.5e-31, 0))
effecttab(rt, tlabels = c("0" = "Nonsmoker", "1" = "Smoker"), method = "ipw")

# Or as a matrix with columns estimate, lower, upper and p-value
m <- matrix(c(0.12, 0.05, 0.19, 0.0004,
              -0.03, -0.08, 0.02, 0.25), 2, byrow = TRUE,
            dimnames = list(c("Treated_vs_control", "Dose_high"), NULL))
effecttab(m, effect = "RD")
}
\seealso{
\code{\link[=tt_effect_rows]{tt_effect_rows()}} for the input rows; \code{\link[=regtab]{regtab()}} for
coefficient tables; \code{\link[=as_forest_data]{as_forest_data()}} for plotting.
}
